# WebSocket frame golden vectors (RFC 6455 §5.7 examples + edge cases).
#
# Each line is one complete frame on the wire plus its decoded fields,
# so NET-10.3's incremental frame codec can pin encode AND decode.
#
# Format (fields '|'-separated):
#   <name> | <wire-hex> | <fin> | <opcode> | <masked> | <payload-hex>
#
#   wire-hex     -> the full frame bytes (header + mask key + payload) in hex
#   fin          -> 0 | 1
#   opcode       -> hex nibble: 0=cont 1=text 2=binary 8=close 9=ping a=pong
#   masked       -> 0 | 1  (client->server frames MUST be masked)
#   payload-hex  -> the UNMASKED application payload bytes in hex
#                   (empty payload written as the token <EMPTY>)
#
# RFC 6455 5.7 worked examples:
#   "Hello" unmasked text  -> 81 05 48 65 6c 6c 6f
#   "Hello" masked  text   -> 81 85 37fa213d 7f9f4d5158  (mask 0x37fa213d)
# plus a 256-byte (16-bit length) frame, ping/pong/close control
# frames, and a 2-frame fragmentation pair (text fragment, then a
# continuation frame with FIN set).
text-unmasked-hello | 810548656c6c6f | 1 | 1 | 0 | 48656c6c6f
text-masked-hello   | 818537fa213d7f9f4d5158 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 48656c6c6f
ping-empty          | 8900 | 1 | 9 | 0 | <EMPTY>
pong-empty          | 8a00 | 1 | a | 0 | <EMPTY>
close-normal        | 880203e8 | 1 | 8 | 0 | 03e8
binary-fragment-1   | 0203414243 | 0 | 2 | 0 | 414243
binary-fragment-2   | 8003444546 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 444546
text-16bit-len      |  | 1 | 1 | 0 | 
